Date: Fri, 26 Mar 1993 13:34:50 -0500
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@snark.shearson.com>
Subject: PHIL: Are we hypocrites? 
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

Garrett Goebel says:
> 
>    I'd like to thank those who responded.
> Their criticisms were insightful... and
> pointed out exactly the weakest areas of
> my argument.  Namely, the things I am
> trying to nail down for myself:
> 
> o  What defines AC/libertarianism/etc... what are the root principles?

Libertarianism is a very broad sort of political philosophy. One can
hold many different positions on life and the world and still be a
libertarian. The only decent way to define a libertarian is as a
person who believes in the non-coercion principle, which can be
defined many ways. Some would say that it is "the only legitimate
relationships between people are those based on voluntary consent".
Another way of phrasing it is "libertarians are those who oppose the
non-consentual initiation of physical violence against persons or
their property, or the threat of such initiation." This is NOT the
underlying principle behind why libertarians necessarily believe in
their philosophy -- it is generally only a way to define what the
results of their beliefs are. Some libertarians, for instance, believe
in moral justifications of one kind or another for their world view.
Some use pragmatic justifications. Some use religious justifications.
The thing that binds them together is, however, that they have all for
one reason or another adopted a variant of the NCP as a product of
their reasoning (or what have you.)

> o  Exactly what kinds of force are allowed vs. what is voluntary?

Generally, this is hard to answer. It depends on the ultimate legal
system you end up adopting. The only point of libertarianism qua
libertarianism is to say "we desire the society where initiation of
violence is rare", not to say what to do about it. Thats where
different approaches to libertarian legal systems come in. Some would
favor turning all the law into civil law, and some would favor being
pacifists, and some would favor a system much like we have now, etc.
Extropians tend to be propertarian anarchist libertarians who favor a
privately produced civil legal system.

> o  Do basic rights exist?  Is there a point where I should be able to
>   force my principles on another? ...where my or their principles are
>   just fundamentally right and merit initiation of force.

These questions are all ill posed. Asking if basic rights exist is
an abuse of language. What does "exist" mean? Do people believe that
they are a good thing? Yes. Do they have the same sort of existance as
the desk my computer is on? No. Define what you mean by exist and
prehaps we can answer this question. 

Then you ask if there is a point where you should be able to force
your principles on others. Well, what does "should" mean? There is no
god to grant or not grant you permission. Obviously the decision to
force or not force your principles on others is your own and has to
come from your own decisions -- others can tell you what they think,
but you are operating as if there is some absolute list of what is
"right" and what is "wrong" written in the stars. Now, I believe that
TO ME certain things are right and wrong, but when you ask, without
context, if there is a point where you "should be able" to force your
principles on others, well, at that point you asking another question
without referents.

Perry

