Date: Fri, 26 Mar 93 12:19:41 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Schelling Points

> Maybe I shouldn't ask, but--what are "schelling points"?
> 
> 
> -- alan (lovejoy@alc.com)

Tom Schelling is/was an economist/game theorist who has studied
political economy, choices theory, etc. His 1960 collection of essays,
"Strategy and Conflict," introduced his notion of "focal points," or
Schelling points.

A Schelling point is a way for players in a game (participants in some
process) to coordinate their expectations (or plans) without prior
communication. 

If Alice and Bob were to agree to meet in Washington, D.C. at some
time, but the place was left unspecified, what would be the places
they'd try? Perhaps in front of the White House, perhaps at the Air
and Space Museum. But my guess is: at the base of the Washington
monument...tallest structure in D.C., visible from everywhere, etc.
Certainly these are more likely than, say, on a side street somewhere.

Or, suppose the place was specified but the time was not. "Noon" has
more "focal power" than, say, 10:43 a.m.

The implications for economics and game theory are intriguing.
Strategies may emerge even when prior arrangement is impossible.

(Another way to view Schelling points, I suspect, is in terms of
Kolmogorov-Chaitin complexity. To wit, it takes fewer bits to specify
"Noon, Washington monument" than most other addresses. In a sense, the
Schelling points are "less random" and have "less entropy" than other
points. This is intimately connected with the issues of what
constitutes an "interesting" hand in poker, a common example of
probability in action.)

-Tim


