Date: Fri, 26 Mar 93 22:07:27 EST
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: MEME: Sociobiology

Thomas D. Burns writes:
> My impression is that sociobiology attempts to explain behavior strictly
> in terms of genetics, downplaying cognition and culture. Anybody have a 
> further clarification?

Some further notes:

The term "sociobiology" is from the title of a book by E. O. Wilson,
who studied ants, and was an early proponent of the idea that the
social structures and behavior of ants could be studied in a Darwinist
way.  (Not too controversial.)  Later, he extended the idea quite
strongly to human behavior, leading to a nearly genetic-determinist
point of view.  (My father once paraphrased him thus: "If we really
understood genetics, we would know why Beethoven's Ninth Symphony
sounds so good.")  Needless to say, this drove the neo-liberals who
dominated the social sciences crazy, since they believed in an
absolutely cultural-determinist point of view.  (This started in the
mid-70s.)

Dale

